@claim q/gb17287cebb93-8-1 { status: inferred, grounds: [d/gb17287cebb93-8], "code:kind": anticipated, "ingest:quote": "pub const SYSTEM: &str = \"You check one claim about a Rust codebase against facts taken from the code.\\n\\\nThe facts are `F1`, `F2`, … (what the code does) and `P1`, `P2`, … (what the code says about itself: doc comments, string literals, string constants).\\n\\\nBreak the claim into its parts — each thing it asserts — and for each part say whether the facts show it. Cite the facts you used by name.\\n\\\nRules you must follow:\\n- Cite only facts you were shown. Do not recall anything about this project from memory.\\n- A part no fact shows is uncovered. Saying so is the useful answer; guessing is not.\\n- If a fact shows the claim is false, set `contradicted` and cite it.\\n- `P` facts are what someone wrote, not what the code does: they may point at the right place, and they never establish a part on their own.\\n\\\nReturn one JSON object: {\"covered\": [{\"part\": string, \"facts\": [string]}], \"uncovered\": [string], \"contradicted\": boolean, \"contradicted_by\": [string]}\";" }
~ The model will receive a clear instruction on how to check one claim against facts, including the distinction …

The model will receive a clear instruction on how to check one claim against facts, including the distinction between structural facts and prose.

